[{"location":"api.html#reference","page":"API","title":"Reference","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html#Contents","page":"API","title":"Contents","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html","page":"API","title":"API","text":"Pages = [\"api.md\"]","category":"page"},{"location":"api.html#Index","page":"API","title":"Index","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api.html","page":"API","title":"API","text":"Pages = [\"api.md\"]","category":"page"},{"location":"api.html","page":"API","title":"API","text":"Modules = [GenerativeModelProtocols]\nPrivate = true ","category":"page"},{"location":"api.html#GenerativeModelProtocols.DiffusionModel","page":"API","title":"GenerativeModelProtocols.DiffusionModel","text":"struct DiffusionModel <: GenerativeModelProtocols.AbstractGenerativeModel\n\nA structure containing the general parameters needed to evaluate and train  a Diffusion Model (DM). Once trained, its denoiser model can be used as a  generative model.\n\nT::Int64: Number of diffusion steps (number of steps in the Markov chain).\nα::Vector{Float64}: Float64(1.0) .- β\nᾱ::Vector{Float64}: cumprod(α)\nβ::Vector{Float64}: Variance of the Gaussian noise added in each diffusion step.\ndenoiser_model::Flux.Chain: Neural network parametrizing the denoising model, that is, a model that probabilisitically undoes the Gaussian noise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#GenerativeModelProtocols.GenerativeModelProtocol","page":"API","title":"GenerativeModelProtocols.GenerativeModelProtocol","text":"struct GenerativeModelProtocol{M<:GenerativeModelProtocols.AbstractGenerativeModel}\n\nA protocol containing a user-specified generative model, and all of its  parameters needed to train it and evaluate it. By default the training and evaluation of the generative model is done in the CPU, however, this can be  changed by setting device to a GPU device of preference.\n\ntraining_data::Matrix{Float64}: Vector containing all the data that is to be used for training.\nepochs::Int64: Number of training epochs for the generative model.\nbatchsize::Int64: Batch size for training the generative model.\nshuffle::Bool: Flag indicating whether to shuffle the training data during training.\noptimiser::Union{Flux.Optimise.AbstractOptimiser, Optimisers.AbstractRule}: Optimizer used to train the generative model.\ndevice::MLDataDevices.AbstractDevice: Hardware device (CPU or GPU) on which to perform training and inference.\nmodel::GenerativeModelProtocols.AbstractGenerativeModel: Generative model architecture to be used.\n_log::GenerativeModelProtocols.TrainingLog: Stores the time-series data generated while training the generative model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api.html#GenerativeModelProtocols.VariationalAutoencoder","page":"API","title":"GenerativeModelProtocols.VariationalAutoencoder","text":"struct VariationalAutoencoder <: GenerativeModelProtocols.AbstractGenerativeModel\n\nA structure containing the general parameters needed to evaluate and train  a Variational Autoencoder (VAE). Once trained, its decoder can be used as a  generative model.\n\nlatent_dim::Int64: Dimensionality of the latent space representation.\nlatent_layers::Int64: Number of latent layers in the encoder and decoder chains of the VAE.\nencoders::Tuple{Vararg{Flux.Chain}}: Encoder chain of the VAE, responsible for encoding time-series data into a latent representation.\ndecoders::Tuple{Vararg{Flux.Chain}}: Decoder chain of the VAE, responsible for generating time-series data from the latent representation.\nβ::Union{Float64, Vector{Float64}}: Weighting factor for the KL divergence term in the VAE loss function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"generative_models/diffusion_models.html#Diffusion-Models","page":"Diffusion Models","title":"Diffusion Models","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/diffusion_models.html#Diffusion-Models","page":"Diffusion Models","title":"Diffusion Models","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generative_models/variational_autoencoders.html#Variational-Autoencoders","page":"Variational Autoencoders","title":"Variational Autoencoders","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generative_models/variational_autoencoders.html","page":"Variational Autoencoders","title":"Variational Autoencoders","text":"Variational Autoencoders (VAEs) are among the simplest generative models capable  of learning an unknown probability density distribution from a data collection.  Generally, a VAE consists of two primary components: an encoder and a  decoder. The goal of the encoder is to map a recorded observation to a  lower-dimensional latent variable. This latent variable follows a well-known  probability distribution that is easy to sample from. Conversely, the decoder  transforms these sampled latent variables back into the original  higher-dimensional data space.","category":"page"},{"location":"generative_models/variational_autoencoders.html","page":"Variational Autoencoders","title":"Variational Autoencoders","text":"Once a VAE is trained, its decoder can be used as a generative model. Because  the latent space is designed to be simple, generating new random observations  merely requires sampling directly from this distribution. Typically, these  latent variables are optimized to be statistically independent, following a  normal distribution with zero mean and unit variance.","category":"page"},{"location":"generative_models/variational_autoencoders.html","page":"Variational Autoencoders","title":"Variational Autoencoders","text":"<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 1em 0;\">\n    <img src=\"vae_diagram.svg\" alt=\"vae_diagram\" \n        style=\"background-color: white; border: 1px solid black; padding: 10px; \n        width: 100%; max-width: 750px; height: auto;\">\n</div>","category":"page"},{"location":"generative_models/variational_autoencoders.html#Evidence-Lower-Bound","page":"Variational Autoencoders","title":"Evidence Lower Bound","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generative_models/variational_autoencoders.html","page":"Variational Autoencoders","title":"Variational Autoencoders","text":"Before addressing any other training steps, such as optimizer selection, one  must define an appropriate objective function. For deterministic models, this  choice is relatively trivial because intuitive objective functions are  straightforward to formulate. For instance, regression tasks often rely on  Mean Squared Error (MSE), whereas for classification tasks Cross-Entropy loss is often used. For probabilistic models like VAEs, however, the objective  function must be derived from probability theory to handle data distributions  rather than point predictions.","category":"page"},{"location":"generative_models/variational_autoencoders.html","page":"Variational Autoencoders","title":"Variational Autoencoders","text":"As established, the latent variables z of a VAE are stochastic, originating  from an explicitly chosen prior distribution p(z) for which sampling is  tractable. Consequently, we seek to maximize the log-likelihood of our observed  training data x by marginalizing over these latent variables:","category":"page"},{"location":"generative_models/variational_autoencoders.html","page":"Variational Autoencoders","title":"Variational Autoencoders","text":"log left( p (x) right) = log left( int p (xz) mathrmdz right) ","category":"page"},{"location":"generative_models/variational_autoencoders.html","page":"Variational Autoencoders","title":"Variational Autoencoders","text":"However, because the true posterior distribution p(zx) is unknown, this  marginal integral is analytically intractable, approximations must be made.","category":"page"},{"location":"generative_models/variational_autoencoders.html","page":"Variational Autoencoders","title":"Variational Autoencoders","text":"To address this intractability, we introduce a variational distribution  q_phi(zx) parameterized by an encoder neural network with parameters  phi to approximate the true, intractable posterior p(zx). By expanding the  integral with this variational distribution, we can rewrite the log-likelihood  as an expectation:","category":"page"},{"location":"generative_models/variational_autoencoders.html","page":"Variational Autoencoders","title":"Variational Autoencoders","text":"beginalign*\nlog left( p(x) right) = log left( int q_phi(zx) \n    fracp(xz)q_phi(zx) mathrmdz right) 05cm\n= log left( mathbbE_q_phi(zx) left \n    fracp(xz)q_phi(zx) right right)\nendalign*","category":"page"},{"location":"generative_models/variational_autoencoders.html","page":"Variational Autoencoders","title":"Variational Autoencoders","text":"Since the logarithm function is concave, applying Jensen's inequality allows us  to bound the log-likelihood from below, yielding the Evidence Lower Bound  (ELBO):","category":"page"},{"location":"generative_models/variational_autoencoders.html","page":"Variational Autoencoders","title":"Variational Autoencoders","text":"beginalign*\nlog left( p(x) right) geq mathbbE_q_phi(zx) left log left( \n    fracp(xz)q_phi(zx) right) right\n= mathbbE_q_phi(zx) left log left( p(xz) right) - \n    log left( q_phi(zx) right) right 03cm\n= mathbbE_q_phi(zx) left log left( p_theta (xz) right) + \n    log left( p(z) right) - log left( q_phi(zx) right) right 03cm\n= mathbbE_q_phi(zx) left log left( p_theta (xz) right) right \n    - mathbbE_q_phi(zx) left log left( fracq_phi(zx)p(z) \n    right) right 03cm\n= mathbbE_q_phi(zx) left log left( p_theta (xz) right) right \n    - D_textKL left( q_phi(zx)  parallel  p(z) right)\nendalign*","category":"page"},{"location":"generative_models/variational_autoencoders.html","page":"Variational Autoencoders","title":"Variational Autoencoders","text":"Here, p_theta(xvertz) represents the generative model parameterized by a  decoder neural network with weights theta, and D_textKL denotes the  Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence.","category":"page"},{"location":"generative_models/variational_autoencoders.html","page":"Variational Autoencoders","title":"Variational Autoencoders","text":"Finally, because maximizing the ELBO is equivalent to minimizing its negative,  we can jointly train the encoder and decoder neural networks by solving the  following optimization problem:","category":"page"},{"location":"generative_models/variational_autoencoders.html","page":"Variational Autoencoders","title":"Variational Autoencoders","text":"beginmatrix textargmin  phi thetaendmatrix hspace03cm\n    D_textKL left( q_phi(zx)  parallel  p(z) right) - \n    mathbbE_q_phi(zx) left log left( p_theta (xz) right) right ","category":"page"},{"location":"generative_models/variational_autoencoders.html#Implementation-Considerations","page":"Variational Autoencoders","title":"Implementation Considerations","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generative_models/variational_autoencoders.html","page":"Variational Autoencoders","title":"Variational Autoencoders","text":"To practically implement and train a VAE, several critical architectural and  optimization challenges must be addressed:","category":"page"},{"location":"generative_models/variational_autoencoders.html","page":"Variational Autoencoders","title":"Variational Autoencoders","text":"The Reparameterization Trick: Standard stochastic sampling breaks the  computational graph because it is non-differentiable. To allow backpropagation  via reverse-mode automatic differentiation, the stochastic component must be  isolated. This is achieved by defining z through a deterministic mapping  that ingests external noise, typically formulated as z = mu_phi(x) +  sigma_phi(x) odot epsilon, where epsilon sim mathcalN(0 I).\nAnalytical KL Divergence: Evaluating the KL divergence via sampling is  computationally expensive and introduces high variance. To ensure this term  has a tractable, closed-form algebraic solution, the approximate posterior  q_phi(zvertx) and the prior p(z) are conventionally chosen from the  same family, usually multivariate Gaussians with diagonal covariance matrices.\nFormulating the Reconstruction Loss: The expected log-likelihood term  mathbbE_q_phi(zvertx) log p_theta(xvertz) translates  directly into standard loss functions depending on the assumed distribution  of the data. For continuous data modeled as a Gaussian distribution, this term  simplifies exactly to Mean Squared Error (MSE). For binary or normalized data  modeled as a Bernoulli distribution, it simplifies to Binary Cross-Entropy  (BCE).\nHyperparameter Balancing (beta-VAE and KL Vanishing): In practice,  VAEs often suffer from \"KL vanishing,\" an optimization trap where the encoder  collapses to the prior left( D_textKL to 0 right) and the decoder  ignores the latent space entirely. To prevent this, a scaling hyperparameter  beta is introduced to weight the KL term. While beta = 1 satisfies the  strict mathematical derivation of the ELBO, beta is often dynamically  scheduled (KL annealing) or tuned arbitrarily: beta  1 prioritizes sharp  reconstructions, whereas beta  1 enforces stricter latent independence at  the expense of output detail.\nHierarchical Latents: A single layer of latent variables assumes a simple  flat distribution, which often lacks the mathematical flexibility to model  complex data distributions. To increase model capacity, Hierarchical VAEs  (HVAEs) stack latent variables into sequential layers. This structural change  allows the network to partition the representation task, tracking large-scale  structural patterns in the top layers and smaller, detailed variations in the  lower layers.","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/variational_autoencoders.html#Variational-Autoencoders","page":"Variational Autoencoders","title":"Variational Autoencoders","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"CurrentModule = GenerativeModelProtocols","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html#GenerativeModelProtocols.jl","page":"Home","title":"GenerativeModelProtocols.jl","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"A unified Julia framework for building, training, and deploying generative  models. ","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"This library wraps diverse architectures into a consistent  AbstractGenerativeModel interface. Built on top of  Flux.jl, it delivers a developer-friendly experience  with native GPU acceleration and maintainable code.","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html#Key-Features","page":"Home","title":"Key Features","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"Unified Interface: Standardized protocol for all generative architectures.\nFlux Ecosystem: Seamless integration with modern Flux.jl neural networks.\nHardware Accelerated: Out-of-the-box support for execution on GPUs.\nModel Persistence: Built-in serialization mechanisms to save and ","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"resume workflows.","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html#Storage-and-Serialization","page":"Home","title":"Storage & Serialization","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"The package supports saving and loading trained model states for sharing and  reuse. ","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"To keep the core package lightweight, serialization is handled via package  extensions. Loading FileIO.jl and  HDF5.jl in your environment  automatically enables the HDF5 storage backend.","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html#Supported-Architectures","page":"Home","title":"Supported Architectures","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"While the library includes built-in implementations of popular generative  models, it is built for extensibility. You can easily integrate custom  architectures by subtyping the AbstractGenerativeModel interface and  implementing the required protocol methods.","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"The currently built-in generative model architectures are:","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"(Hierarchical) Variational Autoencoders\nDiffusion Models\nGenerative Adversarial Networks\nNormalizing Flows","category":"page"}]
